B153's Nadeo field audit has the same defect B157 found in B138: its JUSTIFIED rows are unverified free-text prose, so a wrong belief becomes a permanent exemption and the audit defends the defect

SELF-REPORTED by lane-enum on merging B157, about its own earlier B153 work.

WHAT B157 FOUND IN B138. B138's audit carried a JUSTIFIED row exempting the exact
literal B157 turned out to be a bug -- and the stated reason WAS the false claim:
"the Nadeo dump identifies return types by numeric id, not text". Measurably
false; 1618 return types were being discarded behind it.

So the audit did not accept the defect DESPITE a justification. It accepted it
BECAUSE OF one. A free-text reason records that somebody was convinced. It never
records that they were right. Once written, the scanner stops asking, and the
wrong belief is now permanent and load-bearing -- strictly worse than no audit,
because an unaudited literal still gets read by the next person to walk past it.

That is the sixth stale Nadeo-dump claim found in this batch, and the only one
that had been formally blessed.

WHY THIS IS A TICKET RATHER THAN A NOTE. B153's audit (also lane-enum's) has the
IDENTICAL structure. Its JUSTIFIED rows are unverified prose. Its READ_CONFIRMED
rows are the only ones that must prove themselves against the source -- those
carry a snippet that has to literally occur, so a claim that stops being true
fails rather than persisting. The mitigation is already in the file; it is simply
not applied to the JUSTIFIED half.

THE WORK:
  1. Enumerate B153's JUSTIFIED rows.
  2. For each, either convert it to a READ_CONFIRMED-style row whose evidence
     must literally occur in the dump or the source, or re-derive the claim and
     record the measurement that supports it.
  3. Any row that can be neither converted nor re-derived is not justified. It is
     unexamined, and should say so in those words -- an honest UNEXAMINED row is
     worth more than a JUSTIFIED one nobody can substantiate, because it invites
     the next reader to look instead of telling them not to.
  4. Apply the same pass to B138's remaining JUSTIFIED rows. B157 removed one of
     them; the others have never been checked and share the provenance of the one
     that was wrong.

SECOND, SMALLER DEFECT FROM THE SAME COMMIT -- worth fixing here. Removing the
bad literal tripped B138's own non-vacuity floor, `found.len() >= 5`, which read
a legitimate improvement as scanner drift. lane-enum retied the floor to
`JUSTIFIED.len()`.

The general rule, and it belongs in docs/verification-hazards.md: A NON-VACUITY
FLOOR WRITTEN AS A HAND-PICKED CONSTANT ERODES. Every genuine improvement to the
codebase pushes the real count toward it, so the constant must be edited down --
which trains people to lower a floor whenever it complains, which is exactly how
a floor stops meaning anything. Tie it to something that moves WITH the codebase.

Related: B138 (the stale-binary trap and the JUSTIFIED table), B153 (the audit),
B157 (the discovery). See also the hazards entry on a rejection resting on a
claim about the data rotting as quietly as an acceptance does -- this is that
rule applied to an exemption rather than a conclusion.